WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.885
In school we learn algorithm complexity.

00:00:04.885 --> 00:00:07.770
Big O of n.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.655
Big O of n log n.

00:00:10.655 --> 00:00:13.540
We argue about which sort is faster.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.426
As if the difference between n squared and n log n is what matters.

00:00:16.426 --> 00:00:19.311
Then you walk into a real database with a billion tuples.

00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:22.196
And none of that analysis predicts what actually happens.

00:00:22.196 --> 00:00:25.081
The bottleneck is somewhere else.

00:00:25.081 --> 00:00:27.800
Two worlds.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.518
In small data, everything fits in memory.

00:00:30.518 --> 00:00:33.237
CPU is the bottleneck.

00:00:33.237 --> 00:00:35.955
Big O of n versus n squared matters.

00:00:35.955 --> 00:00:38.674
In big data, the data is bigger than RAM.

00:00:38.674 --> 00:00:41.392
It lives on disk.

00:00:41.392 --> 00:00:44.111
Now CPU is essentially free.

00:00:44.111 --> 00:00:46.829
The bottleneck is moving data between disk and CPU.

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:49.548
A CPU comparison takes a nanosecond.

00:00:49.548 --> 00:00:52.266
A disk reed takes ten milliseconds.

00:00:52.266 --> 00:00:54.985
A factor of ten million.

00:00:54.985 --> 00:00:57.703
Skipping one disk reed is worth a thousand CPU operations.

00:00:57.703 --> 00:01:00.422
The two worlds have different physics.

00:01:00.422 --> 00:01:03.876
The unit the disk likes to move is a page.

00:01:03.876 --> 00:01:07.330
Sixty-four kilobytes, give or take.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.784
You do not reed a single tuple off disk.

00:01:10.784 --> 00:01:14.238
You reed the whole page.

00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:17.692
So the cost model becomes simple.

00:01:17.692 --> 00:01:21.146
C sub r times pages reed, plus C sub w times pages written.

00:01:21.146 --> 00:01:24.600
Everything else, the comparisons, loops, arithmetic, is essentially nothing.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.054
Count pages and you have ranked the algorithms correctly.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:31.695
Example one.

00:01:31.695 --> 00:01:35.336
An algorithm that reeds every page once, then writes every page once.

00:01:35.336 --> 00:01:38.976
Cost: C sub r times P of R, plus C sub w times P of R.

00:01:38.976 --> 00:01:42.617
The simplest baseline.

00:01:42.617 --> 00:01:46.258
Any algorithm that touches every page exactly twice lives here.

00:01:46.258 --> 00:01:49.584
Example two.

00:01:49.584 --> 00:01:52.910
A multi-pass algorithm.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:56.237
Reeds the same pages seven times, writes them three times.

00:01:56.237 --> 00:01:59.563
Cost: C sub r times seven P of R, plus C sub w times three P of R.

00:01:59.563 --> 00:02:02.889
Still linear in table size.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:06.215
Just a bigger constant.

00:02:06.215 --> 00:02:09.542
The constant is the number of passes.

00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:12.868
Saving one pass is the single biggest optimization for sort and join algorithms.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:16.251
Example three.

00:02:16.251 --> 00:02:19.635
A logarithmic algorithm.

00:02:19.635 --> 00:02:23.018
Reeds P of R pages log P of R times.

00:02:23.018 --> 00:02:26.402
Writes a small fraction times the tuple count.

00:02:26.402 --> 00:02:29.785
The log term shows up whenever you walk a search tree, like a B-plus-tree index lookup.

00:02:29.785 --> 00:02:33.169
The writes per tuple look bad on paper.

00:02:33.169 --> 00:02:36.552
They are usually trivial because they hit buffer pages, not the disk directly.

00:02:36.552 --> 00:02:40.087
Next time you optimize a query, do not think about big O of n.

00:02:40.087 --> 00:02:43.623
Think about how many pages each algorithm touches.

00:02:43.623 --> 00:02:47.158
The right algorithm is the one that touches fewer pages.

00:02:47.158 --> 00:02:50.693
Even if the in-memory analysis says it should be slower.

00:02:50.693 --> 00:02:54.228
In big data, the disk wins every argument.

00:02:54.228 --> 00:02:57.763
The way to make the disk lose is to give it fewer pages to fetch.

00:02:57.763 --> 00:03:01.299
That is what the next set of algorithms is about.

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:04.834
Hashing first.
